```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc5
diagnosis_text:
PC5 has been misconfigured with IP address 172.16.10.4/24 instead of its
correct address 172.16.10.5/24. As a result, two distinct hosts on the same
Layer-2 segment (switch1, VLAN 10 / 172.16.10.0/24) are simultaneously
claiming the same IP address:

  • pc4  — MAC 22:6e:7d:f4:34:de — 172.16.10.4/24  (correct)
  • pc5  — MAC 66:1f:0f:41:6e:b4 — 172.16.10.4/24  (duplicate — should be .5)

Both hosts answer ARP requests for 172.16.10.4. Every time any neighbour
(the gateway router0 at 172.16.10.1, pc4 itself, or any other host) sends a
gratuitous ARP or responds to an ARP probe, the ARP cache on the receiving
device overwrites its mapping for 172.16.10.4 with whichever MAC responded
most recently. This produces the observed "ARP flipping" between the two MAC
addresses. Traffic destined to 172.16.10.4 is therefore delivered
non-deterministically to whichever host "won" the last ARP race, causing
intermittent connectivity failures for pc4 (traffic diverted to pc5) and for
pc5 itself (its reply traffic is returned to pc4's MAC).

Root cause: pc5's startup configuration assigns it the IP 172.16.10.4/24
instead of 172.16.10.5/24, creating an IP address conflict with pc4 on the
shared VLAN 10 segment.

Corrective fix: Reconfigure pc5's eth0 address to 172.16.10.5/24 (with
default gateway 172.16.10.1). After the change, flush the ARP caches on
router0 and any affected hosts (`ip neigh flush dev <iface>`) to clear stale
duplicate entries immediately.
=== END ===
```